-- Backward through the concept layer: an edited profile part -> the rule-model edits that would produce it.
-- Binds $model (the rule model the part was written from), $part (APPLICABILITY, DECISION BOUNDARY, MET,
-- NOT MET or UNCERTAIN), $old and $new (that part's text before and after a person edited it) and $p (the
-- rule's packet). Replies with node edits: change a node's text, add a node, or remove one, each with the
-- packet passage it rests on quoted exactly, or an empty source when the packet does not imply it. Applying
-- them is deterministic; the forward pass then re-derives every part from the edited model.
$json%($parse%(=>("You are computing a BACKWARD pass through a rule model. A program wrote one part of an evaluation profile from the RULE MODEL below: MET holds when the action occurs, every condition holds and no permission applies; NOT MET is the exact complement; UNCERTAIN names facts that decide between them. A person then edited that part. Find the smallest edits to the RULE MODEL that would make the program write the edited part, so that the other parts can be re-derived consistently.\n\nRules:\n- Change the model, not the wording: if the edit only rephrases, return no edits.\n- An added exception is a new permission; a new requirement for MET is a new condition; a narrowed or widened condition is an edit of that condition's text; a new deciding unknown is a new fact; a reversed condition is an edit of its text.\n- Give each edited or added node the packet passage it rests on, quoted exactly from the PACKET, or an empty source if the packet does not imply it.\n\nReply with JSON only: {\"edits\": [{\"op\": \"edit\" | \"add\" | \"remove\", \"group\": \"action\" | \"conditions\" | \"permissions\" | \"facts\", \"id\": \"<the node's id, for edit and remove>\", \"text\": \"<the node's new text, for edit and add>\", \"source\": \"<packet passage or empty>\"}], \"why\": \"<one sentence>\"}\n\nRULE MODEL:\n" ++ $json%$model ++ "\n\nPACKET:\n" ++ $json%$p ++ "\n\nPART: " ++ $part ++ "\nBEFORE: " ++ $old ++ "\nAFTER: " ++ $new)))
